  <header class=3D"post-header">
    <h1 class=3D"post-title p-name" itemprop=3D"name headline">Edge River M=
ethod Part 1 of ???: Introduction (and some Theory)</h1>
    <p class=3D"post-meta">
      <time class=3D"dt-published" datetime=3D"2026-06-08T04:00:00+00:00" i=
temprop=3D"datePublished">Jun 8, 2026
      </time></p>
  </header>

  <div class=3D"post-content e-content" itemprop=3D"articleBody">
   =20

<h2 id=3D"fundamentals">Fundamentals</h2>

<p>Yes, there=E2=80=99s already a <a href=3D"https://origami.abstreamace.co=
m/2021/12/12/geography-of-origami-a-brief-introduction-to-the-edge-river-me=
thod/">blog post about ERM</a>,
but there=E2=80=99s<sup id=3D"fnref:is" role=3D"doc-noteref"><a href=3D"htt=
ps://www.hayastl.com/2026/06/08/erm-01-introduction.html#fn:is" class=3D"fo=
otnote" rel=3D"footnote">1</a></sup> only one blog post about it and appare=
ntly ERM resources are obscure. So here=E2=80=99s another one, since I rece=
ntly delved into some specific aspects of ERM.</p>

<p>The <em>edge river method (ERM)</em> is a method that=E2=80=99s pretty s=
imilar to the axial method, but meant to extend to general 2D polygons. Let=
=E2=80=99s take a look at a classic axial packing
map.</p>
<div class=3D"figrow">
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 400px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-01-introduction/axial-abstraction.svg">
        <figcaption>The tree, with edges labelled.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"min-width: 400px; max-width: 400px;" src=3D"https://w=
ww.hayastl.com/assets/posts/erm-01-introduction/axial-packing.svg">
        <figcaption>The map. Dotted black lines are ridge lines.</figcaptio=
n>
    </figure>
</div>

<p>Yep, just a regular axial map. It has some rivers, and they turn. One th=
ing you can do here is to place edges of the tree directly onto the axial p=
acking.</p>
<div class=3D"figrow">
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"min-width: 400px; max-width: 400px;" src=3D"https://w=
ww.hayastl.com/assets/posts/erm-01-introduction/axial-packing-sources.svg">
        <figcaption>The map after placing edges onto the packing. Flap F ha=
s been turned into a middle flap for illustration purposes.</figcaption>
    </figure>
</div>

<p>Notice that each river comes out of an edge and flows and turns until it=
 reaches the boundary of the paper or the same edge from the other side (as=
 in river F).<sup id=3D"fnref:belt" role=3D"doc-noteref"><a href=3D"https:/=
/www.hayastl.com/2026/06/08/erm-01-introduction.html#fn:belt" class=3D"foot=
note" rel=3D"footnote">2</a></sup>
This concept can be generalized to the case where the =E2=80=9Ctree=E2=80=
=9D is actually a shape made of 2D polygons (and from now on will be called=
 an <em>abstraction</em><sup id=3D"fnref:fat" role=3D"doc-noteref"><a href=
=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/2026/06/08/erm-01-introduction.html#fn:fat" cla=
ss=3D"footnote" rel=3D"footnote">3</a></sup>)
Let=E2=80=99s take a look at a sample ERM map that I made.</p>

<div class=3D"figrow">
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 400px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-01-introduction/tadashi-abstraction.svg">
        <figcaption>The abstraction.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 800px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-01-introduction/tadashi-erm.svg">
        <figcaption>The ERM map.</figcaption>
    </figure>
</div>

<p>From each edge comes a river, which flows until it reaches the boundary =
of the paper or another edge that ends up in the same position.
For example, the bottom middle river coming from region A flows until it re=
aches the top edge of region B, which ends up at the same position.
In general, an ERM map has several components (names given by Mu-Tsun Tsai)=
</p>
<ul>
  <li>
<strong>Lakes</strong> (blue). These are the blocks that make up the abstra=
ction and need to be packed into the map.</li>
  <li>
<strong>Backside Lakes</strong> (dark blue). These are lakes that end up on=
 the back side. They can be explicitly planned, or they can just show up wh=
en rivers intersect, as in this case, where the bottom-right river and the =
right-side river from region A intersect. (Regular lakes will be called <em=
>frontside lakes</em> if they need to be distinguished from backside lakes.=
)</li>
  <li>
<strong>Rivers</strong> (striped green). These are similar to rivers in an =
axial packing. They enforce the condition on the distance between the corre=
sponding endpoints on the lake, and are mostly not allowed to cross each ot=
her (though sometimes they can, creating more lakes. We=E2=80=99ll get into=
 that later.) When folding the crease pattern, the entire river gets folded=
 onto roughly the same edge.</li>
  <li>
<strong>Lands</strong> (background color). Any area that=E2=80=99s not a la=
ke or a river.</li>
  <li>
<strong>Peninsulas</strong><sup id=3D"fnref:nml" role=3D"doc-noteref"><a hr=
ef=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/2026/06/08/erm-01-introduction.html#fn:nml" c=
lass=3D"footnote" rel=3D"footnote">4</a></sup> (diamond red). A type of lan=
d that=E2=80=99s a feature unique to ERM. In general, rivers that come out =
of a concavity
(e.g. the concavity between the bottom-left of region A and the left of reg=
ion B) cannot share an edge (we=E2=80=99ll get into why later). Peninsulas =
are explicitly marked as a reminder of this constraint, as it is one of the=
 most important facts about ERM and easy to forget when constructing a pack=
ing.</li>
  <li>the <strong>Sea</strong> (outside the diagram). This is the paper bou=
ndary.</li>
</ul>

<p>One of the most important differences between regular axial packing and =
ERM is literally just that, ERM is not axial. In axial packing, the =E2=80=
=9Cabstraction=E2=80=9D lies on a single axis,
so all rivers collapse to the same direction. But in ERM, rivers can collap=
se to different directions,
which is responsible for basically all the complications of ERM compared
to axial packing.</p>

<p>So, now you=E2=80=99re ready to start packing with ERM! (on a basic leve=
l):</p>
<ol>
  <li>Come up with an abstraction.</li>
  <li>Pack the abstraction into a map, forming lakes.</li>
  <li>Send a river from each lake edge and make sure it goes to the sea or =
to a lake edge that will end up in the same place (like in axial packing).<=
/li>
  <li>Make sure the rivers don=E2=80=99t intersect (unless you know what yo=
u=E2=80=99re doing)</li>
  <li>If two rivers form a concavity, make sure there=E2=80=99s space betwe=
en them.</li>
  <li>??? (draw a crease pattern)</li>
  <li>Profit!</li>
</ol>

<p>Okay, but if you want to use ERM at a higher level, we need to talk abou=
t ERM theory.</p>

<h2 id=3D"theory">Theory</h2>

<h3 id=3D"lakes-rivers-and-lands">Lakes, Rivers, and Lands</h3>

<p>Fundamentally, ERM is a way of telling the paper where to go to construc=
t the target shape without stretching it or tearing it. In the ideal versio=
n of ERM, where you can make
infinitely dense folds, there are only 3 types of regions: lakes, rivers, a=
nd lands. Let=E2=80=99s take a look at the following example, which shows a=
n abstraction with 3 flaps.</p>

<div class=3D"figrow">
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 400px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-01-introduction/3-flap-abstraction.svg">
        <figcaption>The abstraction, with a region and 3 points labelled.</=
figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 800px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-01-introduction/3-flap-erm.svg">
        <figcaption>The ERM map, which shows where those points go.</figcap=
tion>
    </figure>
</div>

<p>Let=E2=80=99s look at what lakes, rivers, and lands do in this ideal mod=
el:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Lakes are regions that map directly to the abstraction and don=E2=80=
=99t collapse. A point inside a lake (e.g. point <mjx-container class=3D"Ma=
thJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0" jax=3D"CHTML" tabindex=3D"0" ctxtmenu_counter=3D=
"0" style=3D"font-size: 117.4%; position: relative;"><mjx-math class=3D"MJX=
-TEX" aria-hidden=3D"true"><mjx-mi class=3D"mjx-i"><mjx-c class=3D"mjx-c1D4=
43 TEX-I"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=3D"on=
" display=3D"inline"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi=
>P</mi></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container>) turns into a point in t=
he abstraction.</li>
  <li>Rivers are constant-width regions that collapse onto an edge in the a=
bstraction. A line inside a river that is parallel to the river (e.g. line =
<mjx-container class=3D"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0" jax=3D"CHTML" tabindex=
=3D"0" ctxtmenu_counter=3D"1" style=3D"font-size: 117.4%; position: relativ=
e;"><mjx-math class=3D"MJX-TEX" aria-hidden=3D"true"><mjx-mi class=3D"mjx-i=
"><mjx-c class=3D"mjx-c1D444 TEX-I"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-math><mjx-assist=
ive-mml unselectable=3D"on" display=3D"inline"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3=
.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>Q</mi></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container=
>) turns into a point in the abstraction (specifically a point on some edge=
). Rivers contain infinitely dense creases, each one perpendicular to the f=
low of the river.</li>
  <li>Lands are regions that collapse onto a single point in the abstractio=
n. An entire land (e.g. region <mjx-container class=3D"MathJax CtxtMenu_Att=
ached_0" jax=3D"CHTML" tabindex=3D"0" ctxtmenu_counter=3D"2" style=3D"font-=
size: 117.4%; position: relative;"><mjx-math class=3D"MJX-TEX" aria-hidden=
=3D"true"><mjx-mi class=3D"mjx-i"><mjx-c class=3D"mjx-c1D445 TEX-I"></mjx-c=
></mjx-mi></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=3D"on" display=3D"inli=
ne"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>R</mi></math></m=
jx-assistive-mml></mjx-container>) turns into a point in the abstraction (s=
pecifically a vertex).</li>
</ul>

<p>When you make the infinitely dense crimps on the rivers and the double i=
nfinitely dense all-directional crimps on the lands required to collapse th=
is ideal ERM map, you get
exactly the target abstraction. If you could actually do infinitely dense c=
rimps, then this would be the end of it. Lakes! Rivers! Lands! The fundamen=
tal regions of ERM!</p>

<p>Unfortunately, on real paper, you can=E2=80=99t make infinitely dense cr=
eases, and all the complications of ERM past the fundamental regions come f=
rom this fact.</p>

<p>Sometimes, you get lucky and you can just make the infinitely dense crea=
ses finite, and it will fold as intended:</p>
<div class=3D"figrow">
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 400px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-01-introduction/3-flap-cp.svg">
        <figcaption>The 3-flap crease pattern, with the ERM map in the back=
ground</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 400px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-01-introduction/3-flap.jpg">
        <figcaption>The result.</figcaption>
    </figure>
</div>

<h3 id=3D"waffle-walls">Waffle walls</h3>

<p>Unfortunately, you usually don=E2=80=99t get so lucky. Consider the foll=
owing ERM map, which represents a 540=C2=B0 angle made of 6 axis-aligned sq=
uares.</p>

<div class=3D"figrow">
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 400px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-01-introduction/540-abstraction.svg">
        <figcaption>The abstraction.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 800px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-01-introduction/540-erm.svg">
        <figcaption>The ERM map. Note land <mjx-container class=3D"MathJax =
CtxtMenu_Attached_0" jax=3D"CHTML" tabindex=3D"0" ctxtmenu_counter=3D"3" st=
yle=3D"font-size: 117.4%; position: relative;"><mjx-math class=3D"MJX-TEX" =
aria-hidden=3D"true"><mjx-mi class=3D"mjx-i"><mjx-c class=3D"mjx-c1D443 TEX=
-I"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=3D"on" disp=
lay=3D"inline"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>P</mi=
></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container> in the middle.</figcaption>
    </figure>
</div>

<p>Now let=E2=80=99s try to draw a crease pattern for this map. The outer l=
ands and rivers are easy, but land <mjx-container class=3D"MathJax CtxtMenu=
_Attached_0" jax=3D"CHTML" tabindex=3D"0" ctxtmenu_counter=3D"4" style=3D"f=
ont-size: 117.4%; position: relative;"><mjx-math class=3D"MJX-TEX" aria-hid=
den=3D"true"><mjx-mi class=3D"mjx-i"><mjx-c class=3D"mjx-c1D443 TEX-I"></mj=
x-c></mjx-mi></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=3D"on" display=3D"i=
nline"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>P</mi></math>=
</mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container> looks tricky.
Instead of using <a href=3D"https://erikdemaine.org/papers/Origamizer_SoCG2=
017/paper.pdf">Origamizer</a> since I=E2=80=99m not Tomohiro Tachi, let=E2=
=80=99s try to do it more manually.
A first attempt is shown below<sup id=3D"fnref:3d" role=3D"doc-noteref"><a =
href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/2026/06/08/erm-01-introduction.html#fn:3d" =
class=3D"footnote" rel=3D"footnote">5</a></sup>:</p>

<div class=3D"figrow">
    <figure>
        <div>
<img style=3D"min-width: 400px; max-width: 400px;" src=3D"https://www.hayas=
tl.com/assets/posts/erm-01-introduction/540-level-0.fold.d.svg" class=3D"fo=
ld">
<div class=3D"fold-options" style=3D"display: none;">
<div><a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/assets/posts/erm-01-introduction/54=
0-level-0.fold">.fold</a></div>
<div><a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/assets/posts/erm-01-introduction/54=
0-level-0.fold.cp">.cp</a></div>
<div><a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/assets/posts/erm-01-introduction/54=
0-level-0.fold.svg">.svg</a></div>
</div>
</div>
        <figcaption>A first attempt at the 540=C2=B0 angle. Faded lines are=
 folded less than 180=C2=B0.</figcaption>
    </figure>
</div>

<p>But now let=E2=80=99s actually try to fold it.</p>

<div class=3D"figrow">
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 400px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-01-introduction/540-front.jpg">
        <figcaption>The front side. Perfect!</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 400px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-01-introduction/540-back-level-0.jpg">
        <figcaption>The back side. Hmm, there's something sticking out of t=
he vertex...</figcaption>
    </figure>
</div>

<p>Most of the paper on the back side is aligned with the abstraction, but =
there=E2=80=99s a 6-sided-star-shaped wall sticking out. This is what I lik=
