<h3 id=3D"theory-summary">Theory Summary</h3>

<p>So, now you=E2=80=99re ready to start packing with ERM! (on a more theor=
etical level):</p>
<ol>
  <li>Come up with an abstraction. Keep in mind that waffle walls are inevi=
table if there=E2=80=99s an internal point that must be winded more than 36=
0=C2=B0 around.</li>
  <li>Pack the abstraction into a map, forming lakes.</li>
  <li>Send a river from each lake edge and make sure it goes to the sea or =
to a lake edge that will end up in the same place.</li>
  <li><em>If two rivers form a concavity, make sure there=E2=80=99s space b=
etween them. Use a peninsula as a reminder.</em></li>
  <li>If two rivers form a convexity, they can intersect as long as distanc=
e constraints are still satisfied. If they cross, beware the river mapping!=
</li>
  <li>Calculate the lakes for river overlaps.</li>
  <li>??? (draw a crease pattern)</li>
  <li>Profit!</li>
</ol>

<h2 id=3D"drawing-a-finite-crease-pattern">Drawing a (Finite!) Crease Patte=
rn</h2>

<p>After you=E2=80=99ve worked long and hard creating that ERM map, you sti=
ll need to fold it. I=E2=80=99ll talk about this briefly.</p>
<ol>
  <li>Discretize the ERM map, because you=E2=80=99re not getting smooth cir=
cle rivers
with a finite number of folds. This can be done by sharpening the circles a=
t specific places called <em>ridge line</em>. In box pleating, for example,=
 rivers turn 90=C2=B0
at a time, except at Pythagorean stretches.</li>
  <li>Fill each land with belts (rivers that just loop on themselves)</li>
  <li>Fill in the rivers with parallel creases alternating mountain and val=
ley<sup id=3D"fnref:alternating" role=3D"doc-noteref"><a href=3D"https://ww=
w.hayastl.com/2026/06/08/erm-01-introduction.html#fn:alternating" class=3D"=
footnote" rel=3D"footnote">13</a></sup>, like in axial design.</li>
  <li>Add in the missing creases. (This is the hard step, and requires some=
 crease manipulation. If a situation looks impossible, it could be because =
you forgot to separate concavity
rivers with a peninsula.)</li>
</ol>

