      <p>Well, I don=E2=80=99t think there=E2=80=99s that many, especially =
ones that use overlapping rivers&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/20=
26/06/08/erm-01-introduction.html#fnref:diagrammers" class=3D"reversefootno=
te" role=3D"doc-backlink">=E2=86=A9</a></p>
    </li>
    <li id=3D"fn:push" role=3D"doc-endnote">
      <p>Well, technically you could, but it makes things inconvenient, and=
 you either get a narrow peninsula or lots of lakes.&nbsp;<a href=3D"https:=
//www.hayastl.com/2026/06/08/erm-01-introduction.html#fnref:push" class=3D"=
reversefootnote" role=3D"doc-backlink">=E2=86=A9</a></p>
    </li>
    <li id=3D"fn:unfair" role=3D"doc-endnote">
      <p>I guess it=E2=80=99s an unfair exercise, since I didn=E2=80=99t ex=
plain how to turn an ERM map into a crease pattern.&nbsp;<a href=3D"https:/=
/www.hayastl.com/2026/06/08/erm-01-introduction.html#fnref:unfair" class=3D=
"reversefootnote" role=3D"doc-backlink">=E2=86=A9</a></p>
    </li>
    <li id=3D"fn:alternating" role=3D"doc-endnote">
      <p>They technically don=E2=80=99t need to alternate, but if you don=
=E2=80=99t know what you=E2=80=99re doing you=E2=80=99ll run into self-inte=
rsection hell.&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/2026/06/08/erm-01-in=
troduction.html#fnref:alternating" class=3D"reversefootnote" role=3D"doc-ba=
cklink">=E2=86=A9</a></p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</div>

  </div>

